<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Casimir II of Łęczyca</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1261–<a href="/years/1294">1294</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Casimir II of Łęczyca (c. 1261–1294) was a Polish prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast dynasty</a>, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-i-of-kuyavia">Casimir I of Kuyavia</a>. After his father's death in 1267 he shared the Duchy of Brześć Kujawski with his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-i-lokietek">Władysław the Elbow-high</a>, and from 1288 he ruled Łęczyca. He was killed fighting Lithuanian raiders at the Battle of Trojanów in 1294.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Casimir was the fourth son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-i-of-kuyavia">Casimir I of Kuyavia</a>, the second from his third marriage, to Euphrosyne, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-i-of-opole">Casimir I of Opole</a>; he was probably named after both his father and his maternal grandfather. On his father's death in 1267 he and his full brothers inherited their share of the lands under the regency of their mother until 1275, when they began to rule jointly. He was Duke of Brześć Kujawski from 1267 and of Dobrzyń from 1275.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1288 the death of his childless half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leszek-the-black">Leszek II the Black</a> brought him the Duchy of Łęczyca in the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. The next year he and his brother Władysław I the Elbow-high backed Bolesław II of Płock's bid for the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, and on 26 February 1289 their forces defeated Silesian troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-glogow">Henry III of Głogów</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolko-i-of-opole">Bolko I of Opole</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/przemko-of-scinawa">Przemko of Ścinawa</a> at the Battle of Siewierz. When Bolesław dropped his claim, Władysław tried to win <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesser-poland">Lesser Poland</a> for himself, with Casimir's close help, in a war against King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-ii-of-bohemia">Wenceslaus II of Bohemia</a>.</p><p>The policy failed in the autumn of 1292, when a Bohemian expedition captured both brothers, and under the peace of 9 October that year they had to pay homage to Wenceslaus. They did not give up. On 6 January 1293 they met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/przemysl-ii">Przemysł II</a> of Greater Poland and Jakub Świnka, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalisz">Kalisz</a> and made a secret agreement for the recovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, pledging 300 pieces of fine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> a year from the salt mines once the city was taken.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 4 June 1294 a Lithuanian force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytenis">Vytenis</a> attacked Łęczyca. According to the chronicler Jan Długosz it numbered 1,800 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a>, Prussians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitians">Samogitians</a>, plundered the collegiate church and carried off many people. Casimir pursued the raiders with his knights and was killed in the fighting on the bank of the Bzura river on 10 June; Długosz writes that the Polish knights then fled in all directions and many drowned in the river. He died unmarried and childless, and Władysław I inherited his duchy. His burial place is unknown, but he was probably buried in the collegiate church of St. Mary and St. Alexius at Tum near Łęczyca.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Casimir II of Łęczyca” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Casimir I died in 1267 his lands were divided among his sons: Leszek the Black received Sieradz, Ziemomysl took the Duchy of Inowroclaw, and Wladyslaw I the Elbow-high and <b>Casimir II</b> shared the Duchy of Brzesc Kujawski.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-kuyavia">Duchy of Kuyavia</a></li></ul></section>
